{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small, independently operated services is often *less* resilient to correlated failure than one large service with strictly more total internal redundancy, because decentralization fragments oversight and makes systemic coupling to a shared root cause (e.g. a hypervisor 0-day, an NTP daemon bug, a shared PKI trust anchor) invisible until cascading failures manifest.","counterconditions":["If the small services are shown to use heterogeneous implementations across the full dependency stack (kernels, runtimes, cloud providers, CAs), the shared-root-cause risk drops and resilience may exceed the monolith.","If a global rollback / kill-switch can be exercised faster by the decentralized network than by the large service, the coordination advantage flips.","If the large service's internal redundancy is uniform rather than diverse (same build, same firmware), it is just as correlated as the decentralized set and the claim collapses to symmetry."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independence of operation does not imply independence of failure. Small operators converge on the same narrow set of cheap, standardized dependencies (kernel, libc, cloud hypervisor, DNS provider, CA, NTP). When a fault is latent in a shared substrate, each node fails independently *in time* but for the *same cause*; the observed non-simultaneity is then mistaken for true independence. The large service, by contrast, can afford heterogeneity budgets, in-house root-cause teams, and coordinated rollback that the small operators cannot.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.62}],"simulation_proposals":[]}